    "Here is the Python code that accomplishes this:\n\n```python\ndef group_states(states):\n    abstract_states = []\n    visited = set()\n\n    def dfs(state, cluster):\n        if state in visited:\n            return\n        visited.add(state)\n        cluster.append(state)\n\n        x, y = state % 10, state // 10\n\n        for dx, dy in [(1, 0), (-1, 0), (0, 1), (0, -1)]:\n            nx, ny = x + dx, y + dy\n            if 0 <= nx < 10 and 0 <= ny < 9:\n                nstate = ny * 10 + nx\n                # skip blocked states\n                if states[nstate] == 'X':\n                    continue\n\n                dfs(nstate, cluster)\n\n    for i in range(len(states)):\n        if states[i] != 'X' and i not in visited:\n            cluster = []\n            dfs(i, cluster)\n            abstract_states.append(cluster)\n\n    return abstract_states\n\n\nstates = [\n    0, 2, 5, 9, 'X', 57, 49, 58, 62,\n    1, 4, 8, 12, 'X', 50, 41, 51, 59,\n    3, 7, 11, 14, 'X', 42, 36, 43, 52,\n    6, 10, 13, 15, 17, 'X', 32, 'X', 56,\n    'X', 'X', 'X', 16, 18, 'X', 27, 'X', 48,\n    63, 60, 53, 'X', 19, 21, 24, 'X', 40,\n    61, 54, 44, 'X', 20, 23, 26, 31, 35,\n    55, 45, 37, 'X', 22, 'X', 30, 'X', 'X',\n    46, 38, 33, 28, 25, 29, 34, 39, 47\n]\n\ngrouped_states = group_states(states)\nprint(grouped_states)\n```\n\nThe code defines a function `dfs` to perform depth-first search and cluster states together. It starts from each unvisited state and explores its neighbors in all four directions (up, down, left, right). If the neighbor is not blocked by an 'X', it recursively calls itself on that neighbor.\n\nPlease note that this solution assumes the input data forms a 10x9 grid where each cell either contains an integer or is blocked ('X').",
